    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Zoroastrianism grew from the hymns of the prophet Zarathustra, who taught that the Wise Lord, Ahura Mazda, calls every person to choose truth over the lie in a cosmic struggle that will end in the triumph of good.</li><li>It was the faith of the Achaemenid kings and the state religion of the Parthian and Sasanian empires, and its ideas of judgement, heaven and hell and a final renewal of the world may have influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</li><li>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muslim-conquest-of-persia">Arab conquest of Iran</a> it became a minority faith; today it survives among small communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parsis">Parsis</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, whose contribution to modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> was out of all proportion to their numbers.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Magi who bring gifts to the infant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> in the Gospel of Matthew bear the title of the priests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, the magi, who were the guardians of the Zoroastrian tradition.</p></aside>
    </section>

