    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great.jpg" alt="Cyrus the Great" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cyrus_II_(The_Great)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Surenae, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Zoroastrianism traces its teaching to the prophet Zarathustra, known to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> as Zoroaster. Almost nothing is certain about his life. The language of the hymns attributed to him, the Gathas, is an archaic form of Avestan, an Iranian language closely related to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a>, and the world they describe is one of herders and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> without cities or kings. For these reasons many scholars place him in the second millennium or the early first millennium BCE, somewhere in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>; others, following a later Zoroastrian tradition, date him to the 7th or 6th century BCE.</p><p>Zarathustra was a priest of the old Iranian religion, which, like the Vedic religion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, honoured many gods and offered sacrifices. According to the Gathas, he received a revelation from Ahura Mazda, the “Wise Lord”, whom he proclaimed as the supreme creator, uncreated and wholly good. He condemned the worship of the daevas, gods whom he regarded as evil, and called people to stand on the side of truth and order, asha, against the lie, druj. His message met hostility at first, but according to tradition he converted a ruler named Vishtaspa, who protected and spread the faith.</p><p>The religion grew among the Iranian peoples, blending Zarathustra’s teaching with older traditions, and by the 6th century BCE it was the faith of the Persian kings. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a>, who ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> from 522 BCE, proclaimed in his inscriptions, above all at Behistun, that he ruled by the will of Ahura Mazda. Whether <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> before him was a Zoroastrian is debated. The Greek historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> described the Persian magi, the priestly class who later became the guardians of Zoroastrian tradition, and their custom of exposing the dead.</p><p>The religion that emerged combined Zarathustra’s vision with ancient rites: the reverence of fire and water as pure creations, the sacrifice performed by priests, and the veneration of beings such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mithra</a>, the god of covenants, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anahita">Anahita</a>, the goddess of the waters, who were brought back into the tradition as worthy beings created by Ahura Mazda.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Practice</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mithra"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mithra.jpg" alt="Mithra" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mithra</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taq-e_Bostan_-_High-relief_of_Ardeshir_II_investiture.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Philippe Chavin, CC BY 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Zoroastrianism teaches that Ahura Mazda is the wise and good creator of all that is good. Opposed to him is Angra Mainyu, the destructive spirit, the source of evil, death and disorder, who is not his equal but his adversary. The world is the scene of the struggle between them, and every human being, endowed with free will, takes part in it through the choices of their thoughts, words and deeds. The ethical motto of the faith is “good thoughts, good words, good deeds” (humata, hukhta, hvarshta).</p><p>Ahura Mazda works through the Amesha Spentas, the “holy immortals”, divine aspects or beings such as Good Mind, Best Truth and Holy Devotion, each linked to a part of creation, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and fire to earth, water and plants. Lesser divine beings, the yazatas, “those worthy of worship”, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mithra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anahita">Anahita</a>, are honoured in prayer.</p><p>Zoroastrianism teaches a personal and a final judgement. At death the soul crosses the Chinvat Bridge, which widens for the righteous, who pass to the “House of Song”, and narrows for the wicked, who fall into the place of the lie. At the end of time a saviour, the Saoshyant, will appear, the dead will rise, evil will be destroyed and the world will be made perfect for ever in a final renewal, the frashokereti.</p><p>Worship centres on purity and on fire, the symbol of Ahura Mazda’s light and truth; Zoroastrians pray facing a source of light, and they do not regard fire itself as a god. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fire-temple">Fire temples</a> keep sacred fires burning continuously, the most important of which have burned for centuries. Priests perform the yasna, the central act of worship, in which the sacred texts are recited and offerings consecrated.</p><p>Every Zoroastrian child is initiated at about seven to fifteen years of age in a ceremony in which they receive the sacred shirt, the sudreh, and the sacred cord, the kusti, which they untie and retie while praying several times a day. Because dead bodies were regarded as polluting, Zoroastrians traditionally exposed the dead to birds in towers of silence, the dakhmas, so as not to defile earth or fire; many now use burial or cremation instead.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="texts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Texts &amp; Teachers</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ardashir-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ardashir-i.jpg" alt="Ardashir I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ardashir-i">Ardashir I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dinar_of_Ardashir_I_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The scripture of Zoroastrianism is the Avesta, a collection of texts in the Avestan language that was transmitted orally for many centuries before being written down. Its oldest part is the Gathas, seventeen hymns traditionally attributed to Zarathustra himself, composed in Old Avestan in a difficult, allusive poetry. Around them stand later texts in Young Avestan: the Yasna, the liturgy of the main act of worship, which contains the Gathas; the Yashts, hymns to individual divine beings; the Vendidad, a book of laws of purity; and prayers for daily use.</p><p>According to later Zoroastrian tradition, a great Avesta of twenty-one books existed under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenids</a> and was destroyed or scattered when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, then gathered again under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a>. Under the Sasanians, probably in the 5th or 6th century CE, priests invented a precise alphabet to write the Avesta down; much of what they recorded was later lost, and the surviving Avesta is perhaps a quarter of the Sasanian collection.</p><p>The Sasanian priesthood also developed a large body of commentary and teaching in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Middle Persian</a>, written in the Pahlavi script. Most of it survives in books compiled in the 9th and 10th centuries, after the Arab conquest, when the priests sought to preserve their tradition: the Denkard, an encyclopaedia of Zoroastrian learning; the Bundahishn, an account of creation and the end of the world; and works of law, apologetics and visions, such as the Book of Arda Viraf, which describes a journey through heaven and hell.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parsis">Parsis</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> later produced texts in Gujarati and Persian, among them the Story of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanjan">Sanjan</a>, the traditional account of their migration from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. In the 18th and 19th centuries European scholars, beginning with the Frenchman Abraham Hyacinthe Anquetil-Duperron, who studied with Parsi priests in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surat">Surat</a> and published a translation of the Avesta in 1771, made the Zoroastrian scriptures known to the West.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/yazd"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yazd.jpg" alt="Yazd" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/yazd">Yazd</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iran_1343_Yazd_(8665215641).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ninara from Helsinki, Finland, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Zoroastrianism spread among the Iranian peoples from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-plateau">Iranian plateau</a> and became the faith of the three great Persian empires. The Achaemenid kings honoured Ahura Mazda, though they tolerated the religions of their many subjects. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a>, who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> from the 3rd century BCE to the 3rd century CE, Zoroastrianism was the dominant religion, practised in many regional forms.</p><p>The Sasanian dynasty, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardashir-i">Ardashir I</a> in 224, made it a state church. The high priest Kartir, in inscriptions of the late 3rd century, boasted of founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fire-temple">fire temples</a> across the empire and of persecuting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, Christians, Buddhists and Manichaeans. Zoroastrian priests became a powerful hierarchy, closely tied to the monarchy, and the Sasanian state presented itself as the defender of the good religion against Roman and Christian rivals.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muslim-conquest-of-persia">Arab conquest of Iran</a> between 633 and 651 ended Zoroastrianism’s position as the religion of the state. Conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> was gradual: for several centuries Zoroastrians remained numerous, especially in the countryside, and continued to produce learned works. Over time, heavy taxation, social pressure and periods of persecution reduced them to small communities, concentrated around the desert cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazd">Yazd</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a>.</p><p>Some Zoroastrians left Iran. According to the Story of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanjan">Sanjan</a>, a group sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, where a local ruler allowed them to settle on condition that they adopt the local language and customs; the traditional dates of their landing, in the 8th to 10th centuries, are uncertain. Their descendants became known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parsis">Parsis</a>, “<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>”. From the 17th century many Parsis moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a>, where under British rule they became leading merchants, shipbuilders, industrialists and philanthropists.</p><p>In the 19th and 20th centuries Parsis and Iranian Zoroastrians emigrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, forming a small, scattered diaspora.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="community">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Community &amp; Society</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/fire-temple"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fire-temple.jpg" alt="Fire temple" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/fire-temple">Fire temple</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zoroastrian_Fire_Temple,_Yazd_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Uploaded to Flickr by Adam Jones(Copied to Wikimedia by nagualdesign), CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Zoroastrian communities have long been organised around their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fire-temple">fire temples</a>, their priests and their councils. The priesthood is hereditary: priests are drawn from priestly families and trained from childhood to memorise and recite the liturgy. The highest grade of fire, the Atash Behram, is consecrated in an elaborate ceremony from sixteen different fires; there are eight in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and one in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazd">Yazd</a>, where the fire is said to have burned since the 5th century.</p><p>Among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parsis">Parsis</a>, community institutions, the Parsi Punchayet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> above all, managed temples, funds, housing and charities, and Parsi philanthropists founded hospitals, schools and libraries that served all of Bombay’s people. Families such as the Tatas, whose founder Jamsetji Tata built the Taj Mahal Palace hotel in Bombay and planned India’s first great steelworks, made the Parsis one of the most prominent communities in India’s modern economy.</p><p>Zoroastrians do not generally seek converts, and among the Parsis only those born to Parsi fathers have traditionally been accepted into the community, rules that have been debated as numbers decline. The community’s population is ageing and shrinking: the Indian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2011 counted about 57,000 Parsis, and the worldwide total is perhaps 100,000 to 200,000, including converts and communities of Iranian Zoroastrians.</p><p>Festivals mark the seasons and the dead. The most widely celebrated festival of Iranian origin, Nowruz, the new year at the spring equinox, was a Zoroastrian feast before it became a festival for all Iranians and many peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>. The towers of silence, still used by some Parsis, have become difficult to maintain with the decline of vultures in India, and many families now choose other rites.</p><p>Under the Islamic Republic of Iran, Zoroastrians are a recognised minority with a reserved seat in parliament.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/dadabhai-naoroji"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dadabhai-naoroji.jpg" alt="Dadabhai Naoroji" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/dadabhai-naoroji">Dadabhai Naoroji</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dadabhai_Naoroji_1889.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Zoroastrianism is one of the oldest living religions and one of the most influential. Its teaching of a single good creator opposed by a destructive spirit, of a personal judgement after death, of heaven and hell, of a saviour and a final resurrection, has led many scholars to argue that it influenced Jewish thought after the Babylonian exile, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> lived under Persian rule, and through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> the ideas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, though the extent of that influence is debated.</p><p>The magi, the priests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, entered the Christian story as the wise men from the east, and Zoroaster became for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> a legendary sage of wisdom and astrology. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manichaeism">Manichaeism</a>, founded in Sasanian Iran in the 3rd century, borrowed Zoroastrian ideas of the struggle between light and darkness. In the modern West the prophet’s name was given to Friedrich Nietzsche’s philosophical book Thus Spoke Zarathustra, published in the 1880s, though its ideas are Nietzsche’s own.</p><p>Persian culture carries Zoroastrian traces in its calendar, its festivals, above all Nowruz, and the epic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahnameh">Shahnameh</a> of Ferdowsi, completed around 1010, which retells the legends of ancient Iran. The Faravahar, the winged figure from Achaemenid reliefs, has become a symbol of Iranian identity.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parsis">Parsis</a>, despite their small numbers, played an outsized part in the making of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>: in commerce and industry through families such as the Tatas, in politics through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadabhai-naoroji">Dadabhai Naoroji</a>, an early leader of Indian nationalism and the first Asian elected to the British Parliament, in 1892, and in science, law and the arts.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Zoroastrianism, founded on the teaching of the prophet Zarathustra about the Wise Lord, Ahura Mazda, and the struggle between truth and the lie, was the faith of the Persian empires from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenids</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a>. After the Arab conquest it declined to small communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parsis">Parsis</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, but its ideas of judgement, salvation and the end of the world may have shaped the religions of the West.</em></p>

